# Converting Between 128-bit Unsigned Integers and Byte Slices in Go

Go doesn't have a built-in 128-bit integer type, so you'll need to use the `math/big` package or create your own implementation with two `uint64` values.

## Using math/big Package

```go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "math/big"
)

func main() {
    // Create a 128-bit integer
    num := new(big.Int)
    num.SetString("340282366920938463463374607431768211455", 10) // Max 128-bit value

    // Convert big.Int to byte slice
    bytes := num.Bytes()
    fmt.Printf("Bytes: %x\n", bytes)

    // Convert byte slice back to big.Int
    result := new(big.Int)
    result.SetBytes(bytes)

    fmt.Printf("Original: %s\n", num.String())
    fmt.Printf("Restored: %s\n", result.String())
}
```

## Using Two uint64 Values

If you need a custom implementation for performance reasons:

```go
package main

import (
    "encoding/binary"
    "fmt"
)

// Uint128 represents a 128-bit unsigned integer
type Uint128 struct {
    Hi, Lo uint64
}

// ToBytes converts Uint128 to a 16-byte slice
func (u Uint128) ToBytes() []byte {
    bytes := make([]byte, 16)
    binary.BigEndian.PutUint64(bytes[0:8], u.Hi)
    binary.BigEndian.PutUint64(bytes[8:16], u.Lo)
    return bytes
}

// FromBytes converts a 16-byte slice to Uint128
func FromBytes(bytes []byte) Uint128 {
    if len(bytes) < 16 {
        // Pad with zeros if needed
        padded := make([]byte, 16)
        copy(padded[16-len(bytes):], bytes)
        bytes = padded
    }

    return Uint128{
        Hi: binary.BigEndian.Uint64(bytes[0:8]),
        Lo: binary.BigEndian.Uint64(bytes[8:16]),
    }
}

func main() {
    // Create a 128-bit number
    num := Uint128{
        Hi: 0x1234567890ABCDEF,
        Lo: 0xFEDCBA0987654321,
    }

    // Convert to bytes
    bytes := num.ToBytes()
    fmt.Printf("Bytes: %x\n", bytes)

    // Convert back to Uint128
    restored := FromBytes(bytes)
    fmt.Printf("Original: %x %x\n", num.Hi, num.Lo)
    fmt.Printf("Restored: %x %x\n", restored.Hi, restored.Lo)
}
```

This second approach is more efficient for direct 128-bit operations as it avoids the overhead of the `big.Int` package, but provides fewer built-in mathematical operations.
